PREDICT5 -- #530 closing pass 5, Opus seat (2nd instance).  ADDENDUM to PREDICT4 (12:43), which already separated SWAP
from ADD on its own ("RXW SWAPS the direction (count stays 1), RWall ADDS (count becomes 2)") but did not enumerate the
WRITE arm's swap.  The coordinator's correction names it.  Written BEFORE WXR is built or run; mtime is the order proof.

THE SPACE IS EIGHT CELLS: {write arm, read arm} x {DROP, SWAP, DUPLICATE, ADD-OTHER}.

  #  arm    shape   one-edit form                                  name                     status entering this pass
  1  write  DROP    `return 1;' for the forward                    Wdrop                    NEVER BUILT
  2  write  SWAP    `writeflag' -> `MEM_READ'  (ONE TOKEN)         WXR                      NEVER NAMED
  3  write  DUP     a second store forward                         WWall / WWtail / WW6     BUILT THIS PASS -- tails ESCAPE
  4  write  ADD     drop the `return': the read forward also runs  Wpure / W23 / cdxZL3 / dNZtail   in the contract
  5  read   DROP    delete the read forward                        Rdrop                    NEVER BUILT
  6  read   SWAP    `MEM_READ' -> `MEM_WRITE'  (ONE TOKEN)         RXW                      NEVER BUILT
  7  read   DUP     a second read forward                          RD2 / RD2post / RD2z     in the contract
  8  read   ADD     an added MEM_WRITE of the fetched byte         RWall / RWtail           BUILT THIS PASS -- 167/185

PREDICTION FOR THE NEW CELL, WXR -- and the interesting part is that it should be DISTINGUISHABLE from Wdrop row by
row, which is the coordinator's claim and is exactly what I am testing.  The store is lost in BOTH; WXR additionally
performs a bus READ at the port, and on this bus a read is not free.

  row family            Wdrop (no forward at all)              WXR (one READ forward)
  stub<l> w<n>          RED  -- the port is never touched,     GREEN -- one access still happens; the stub counts
                        so the parity does not advance          ACCESSES and is direction-blind
  kbcq * dib            GREEN -- the pre-queued reply          RED  -- the read POPS the queued reply, DIB 0
                        survives an access that never happens
  tailq w t*            GREEN -- same shape                    RED  -- same reason
  iir st*               GREEN -- "TXRDY survives the store"    RED  -- the read CLEARS TXRDY
                        is also true of a store that is lost
  cmos w<n>             RED  -- REG C is never set (0x00)      RED  -- never set AND the read clears it
  store/read-back       RED  -- ofs3 devsig (SCR), split,      RED  -- same; the store is lost either way
  value rows            st4, w2x/w4x/w8x

  So I predict NEITHER escapes, and that the two are told apart by the stub rows and the survives-the-store rows
  moving in OPPOSITE directions.  ** If that holds it settles the coordinator's point with a measurement: a row that
  only checks "the store landed" cannot separate DROP from SWAP; the side-effect rows can. **

  I state no scores.  Two of these four mutants leave `byte' uninitialised and its value is stack residue, so a
  number here would be a guess written in a measured column.

  Rdrop BUILD RISK stands from PREDICT4: the compiler may refuse an uninitialised `byte'.  A BUILD_FAIL is a RESULT
  -- the cell is not reachable by that one-edit form -- and is recorded, not retried away.

WHAT WOULD CHANGE MY VERDICT: any of the four scoring >= 184/185.  I do not expect one.  I am measuring because the
ENUMERATION is the thing under test in this pass, and "it would be caught" is an inference, not a measurement.
